==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{m|la|tam}}:
* with {{reduplication|la|nocap=1}} (earlier {{m|la||*tam-to-}}, or perhaps {{m|la||*tam-tam}} > {{m|la||tantam}}, from which it may be back-formed);
* or from {{der|la|ine-pro|*[[só|teh₂]][[-ónts|-n̥t]][[-os]]}}<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=tam|page=606}}</ref> (compare {{m|la|quantus}});
* or with analogical {{af|la|-tus}} taken from {{m|la|quantus}} (reanalyzed as {{suf|nocat=1|la|quam|-tus}}).
{{rfv-etym|la}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|tantus}}

===Adjective===
{{la-adj|tantus}}

# of such [[size]], of such [[measure]]
# so [[much]], so [[great]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|1|33|quote='''Tantae''' mōlis erat Rōmānam condere gentem.|trans='''''Of so great''' an effort it was to found the Roman people.<br/>or, It was '''so much''' of an effort to found the Roman race.''<br/>(Translations vary of the ironic understatement in this summation of the epic’s opening lines: The gods themselves have conspired and humans suffered to found what will become an empire, i.e., so great the effort, so great the achievement.)}}

====Usage notes====
* Being naturally an adjective, ''tantus'' was then used substantively as ''tantum'' (frequently with genitive) to mean ''"so much of"'', ''"so many of"''; as ''tantī (pretiī)'' to mean ''"so high (a price)"'' [called the genitive of indefinite value]; adverbially as ''tantum'' to mean ''"so much"'', ''"to such degree"'' (cf. {{m|la|tam}}); as ''tantō'' to mean ''"by so much"''. For all these ''quantus'' has its coordinate functions.

